h the  industry perpetually agonizing over its  desire to advance the art of filmmaking  and to spotlight commercial successes  we're going to focus on the history of  the Best Picture by the way because it  can help succinctly shine light on  larger issues in the Academy so  apologies to you best costume design  historians in its first year the Academy  straddled this line by awarding two best  pictures one went to the most  outstanding picture the commercially  successful World War one film wings and  one went to the most unique and artistic  picture the expressionist film sunrise a  song of two humans it created a division  between mainstream and artistic  excellence but then the Academy tweaked  things creating one ultimate Best  Picture category since then that award  has represented a tough balancing act  between entertainment value artisan ship  commercial success in the film  industry's public image all of which  must be reconciled when Hollywood  endorses one film as the definitive best  this has resulted in many questionable  choices in 1953 the still widely  respected films singing in the rain  wasn't even considered for Best Picture  the award instead went to the greatest  show on earth which has an abysmal 43%  critics score on Rotten Tomatoes the  wildly inventive film Brazil a wisecrack  favorite so maybe we're a little biased  also didn't manage to get nominated for  Best Picture in 1986 instead the top  prize went to out of Africa Brazil has a  98% critic approval on Rotten Tomatoes  and a 90% audience approval speaking to  its long lasting appeal and artistic  brilliance  meanwhile Out of Africa has a 58% critic  score and a not so bad 83% audience  score what  more gladiator won best picture in 2000  when the goddamn freaking matrix wasn't  even nominated I'm going to stop this  list before I burst a blood-vessel but I  think the question has been raised why  is the Academy proven to be both  predictable and frankly annoying in its  choice of Best